# SDOC — Implementation Status @sdoc-impl-status
{
    # Meta @meta
    {
        type: doc

        status: active

        sdoc-version: 0.2
    }

    # About @about
    {
        Implementation status for the SDOC project. Tracks what is completed, what is in progress, and what is next. Read this first when starting a new task.
    }

    # Current State @current-state
    {
        SDOC v0.2.22 is the current release. The parser, HTML renderer, slide renderer, slide export pipeline (PDF, PowerPoint / Google Slides) and VS Code extension are stable and published to both npm and the VS Code Marketplace (auto-updates enabled). The format supports scope types, data blocks, comment scopes, table formulas, email auto-detection, collapsible scopes in all HTML output, and a full inline formatting suite. Issues are open on github/markup and github-linguist for GitHub rendering and language recognition.

        The spec is expected to evolve. The core principle of explicit brace scoping is stable. Everything else is open to change, with the constraint that existing documents must remain valid or be mechanically migrateable ([R10](requirements.sdoc#r10)).
    }

    # Completed @completed
    {
        The parser, renderer, and tooling are feature-complete for v0.2. See `specification.sdoc` for the full feature set. Key milestones:

        {[.]
            - v0.2.0 — scope types, data blocks, comment scopes, table width/alignment, key:value meta, line comments, K&R brace style, braceless scopes, implicit root scope
            - v0.2.4 — escaped pipes in table cells
            - v0.2.5 — auto-detected email addresses, file watcher for external preview writes
            - v0.2.6 — table formulas (=SUM, =AVG, =COUNT, arithmetic, A1 references, circular reference detection)
            - v0.2.7 — VS Code Marketplace publishing (entropicwarrior-msenfin.vscode-sdoc), progressive disclosure API documented
            - v0.2.8 — collapsible scopes and copy-to-clipboard in all standalone HTML output
            - v0.2.12 — citations (\`[@key]\` inline references, \`{[citations]\` definition blocks, auto-numbering by order of first appearance, back-links, validation)
            - v0.2.13 — column directives: per-column alignment (\`< > =\`) and number formatting (\`$\`, \`,.N\`, \`.N\`, \`%\`) in table directive rows
            - v0.2.19 — structured slide layouts, theme loading with \`theme.json\`, geometry harvest, PowerPoint / Google Slides export
            - v0.2.21 — optional slides, held back from the formats a deck is sent on in
            - v0.2.22 — drilldown navigation driven by position rather than a build-time class; independent navigation axes (left/right is the spine, up/down the column); deck images resolved against the \`.sdoc\` and embedded in every output format
        }

        # GitHub Adoption
        {
            - [x] Issue opened on github/markup for SDOC rendering support (github/markup#2030)
            - [x] Issue opened on github-linguist for .sdoc language recognition (github-linguist/linguist#7838)
            - [x] sdoc2html CLI translator built as contribution artifact for github/markup
            - [x] llms.txt at repo root for AI agent discovery
        }
    }

    # In Progress @in-progress
    {
        - [ ] Awaiting feedback on github/markup issue (#2030) and github-linguist issue (#7838)
        - [ ] Growing .sdoc file count on GitHub toward linguist recognition threshold (~200 unique repos)

        # Slide Layouts and Export @slides-wip
        {
            Unreleased, on \`feat/slide-layouts\`. Structured slide layouts (\`columns\`,
            \`stats\`, \`pipeline\`, \`matrix\`, \`rows\`, \`bars\`, \`split\`, \`stack\`),
            theme loading with \`theme.json\` and CSS asset inlining, a geometry harvest
            that measures a built deck in headless Chrome, and PowerPoint / Google Slides
            export driven by that measurement. See
            [slide-authoring.sdoc](../docs/reference/slide-authoring.sdoc).

            - [ ] Gate \`--check\` consistently: it is ignored with \`--pdf\` and unconditional with \`--pptx\`, and never sets a non-zero exit code
            - [ ] Decide whether the new modules join the package \`exports\` map or leave the npm tarball — they ship today but are unreachable
            - [ ] Warn when a theme's \`slide\` box and \`page\` size disagree at 96 dpi, instead of silently clipping the PDF
            - [ ] Harvest drops mixed content (text in an element that also has a block child) and \`<br>\` line breaks
            - [ ] No CI runs the test suites; the geometry and PPTX tests skip themselves when Chrome is absent
        }

        # Scatter Layout @scatter-wip
        {
            Merged to \`develop\` and \`main\` on 1 Oct 2026 (PR #64), unreleased —
            no version bump yet. A \`scatter\` layout: labelled
            points on two named axes, the positioning-chart shape. A point
            written entirely in bold is marked, the same semantic rule a
            pipeline step follows, so the slide's argument survives a restyle.
            See [slide-authoring.sdoc](../docs/reference/slide-authoring.sdoc#layout-scatter).

            It is the first layout to place content at coordinates rather than
            in flow, which put two limits of the export path on the record:
            the geometry harvest reads boxes, text and images, so an axis drawn
            in SVG exports as nothing, and nothing anywhere handles rotation,
            so rotated text exports as horizontal text in a tall narrow box.
            Both are now documented under [Writing against the layouts](../docs/reference/slide-authoring.sdoc#theme-classes).

            - [ ] Numeric axes with a stated range, ticks and gridlines are out of scope and would be a separate layout

        # Case-Sensitive Units Under an Uppercasing Theme @units-wip
        {
            Unreleased, on \`feat/slide-background\`. A theme that sets
            \`text-transform: uppercase\` rendered "40 mW" as "40 MW" — a factor
            of 10⁹ on a power figure. The built-in theme does this in ten
            places and the structural CSS in one, so it was not a third-party
            theme's problem.

            The defect class is the interesting part: the document was correct
            and only its rendering was wrong, so no parser, test or gate could
            see it. It was found by a person reading a slide.

            Fixed in the renderer rather than in the theme, because the theme
            is the thing that gets it wrong: a unit symbol following a number
            is wrapped in \`<span class="sdoc-unit">\` and the structural CSS
            opts that span out of case folding. Any theme is protected without
            knowing the guard exists, and it survives PDF and PPTX because the
            harvest reads the computed style per run. Symbols are a closed
            list, and already-capital symbols are left unmarked so no text run
            is fragmented for nothing. See
            [slide-authoring.sdoc](../docs/reference/slide-authoring.sdoc#theme-units).

            - [ ] The same guard is not applied by the document renderer in \`sdoc.js\`; no shipped document CSS uppercases prose today, so nothing is broken, but a custom stylesheet could reintroduce it
        }

        # Slide Background Images @background-wip
        {
            Unreleased, on \`feat/slide-background\`. \`background:\` and its five
            companions put an image behind any slide: \`background-position:\`,
            \`background-size:\`, \`background-scale:\`, \`background-flip:\` and
            \`background-fade:\`. Common keys, so every layout gets them. See
            [slide-authoring.sdoc](../docs/reference/slide-authoring.sdoc#backgrounds).

            Three decisions worth keeping:

            {[.]
                - The image is an \`<img>\`, not a CSS background. That is what makes it embed against the \`.sdoc\`, survive the geometry harvest and export to PowerPoint as a picture shape. A CSS background would build fine and then vanish from the \`.pptx\`.
                - The fade is a mask on the wrapper rather than a coloured scrim, so one fade works on a white deck and a near-black one without the renderer knowing the theme's ground colour — and because it sits on the wrapper, flipping the image never drags the fade off the text it was protecting.
                - \`background:\` only takes the line when the value names an image. "Background: we started in 2019." is a sentence a deck really contains, and swallowing it would delete it and then try to load it as a picture. Generalised as \`VALUE_SHAPES\` beside \`BOOLEAN_KEYS\`.
            }

            - [ ] The PPTX export carries the image but not the fade: the harvest reads boxes, text and images, and a mask is none of those, so the picture arrives at full strength. Exporting it would mean teaching the harvest to read gradients and the exporter to write \`a:gradFill\`.
            - [ ] No way to confine a background to a sub-rectangle of the slide — it is full-bleed, softened by the fade. A hard-edged panel occupying part of the slide would need a further key.

            \`background-size:\` also takes a pair, as in \`auto 100%\`, for the
            shapes no fit keyword can say. A pair sizes the image's own box and
            the background-position rule then places the whole picture, which
            is a second placement path beside \`object-position\` — kept to the
            pair alone, so the keyword path is untouched. A scale folds into
            the pair rather than becoming a transform, so nothing has to
            reconcile a scale origin against the placement offset.
        }
    }

    # Next Up @next-up
    {
        These are agreed tasks ready to be picked up, not a full backlog.

        # Parser Improvements
        {
            - [ ] Enforce single root scope (grammar says `document = scope` but parser allows multiple top-level scopes). Parser should warn on violation. See [C1](requirements.sdoc#c1).
            - [ ] Decouple rendering from AST. Current renderer functions are HTML-centric. Need format-neutral AST with separate renderer modules. See [C2](requirements.sdoc#c2).
        }

        # Extension Improvements
        {
            - [ ] Preview should auto-update when switching active editor tabs (currently requires re-running the preview command)
        }

        # Spec Additions
        {
            - [ ] Document metadata ([S2](suggestions.sdoc#s2)) -- key:value meta is implemented; remaining work is defining canonical well-known keys and exposing `meta.properties` to downstream consumers
        }

        # Export Pipeline
        {
            - [ ] SDOC to Google Docs export (for collaboration with non-technical people)
            - [ ] SDOC to DOCX export
            - [ ] Simple Google Docs / DOCX to SDOC import (for round-tripping simple documents)
        }
    }
}
